{"name":"Salt Lake","hex":"#74c6d3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Salt Lake","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Salt Lake City (a city in Utah, United States).","Salt Lake City, Utah."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Salt_Lake"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salt flat","blocks":[{"text":"Natural salt flats or salt pans are flat, open expanses of ground covered with salt and other minerals, usually shining white under the sun. They are found in deserts and are natural formations."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/20170809_Bolivia_1521_Uyuni_sRGB_%2837926739616%29.jpg/330px-20170809_Bolivia_1521_Uyuni_sRGB_%2837926739616%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_flat"},{"title":"Salt lake","blocks":[{"text":"A salt lake or saline lake is a landlocked body of water that has a concentration of salts and other dissolved minerals significantly higher than most lakes. In some cases, salt lakes have a higher concentration of salt than sea water; such lakes can also be termed hypersaline lake, and may also be pink lakes on account of their color. An alkalic salt lake that has a high content of carbonate is sometimes termed a soda lake."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Water_salinity_diagram.png/330px-Water_salinity_diagram.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_lake"},{"title":"Salt","blocks":[{"text":"Salt is a mineral composed primarily of sodium chloride (NaCl). When used in food, especially in granulated form, it is more formally called table salt. In the form of a natural crystalline mineral, salt is also known as rock salt or halite. Salt is essential for life in general, and saltiness is one of the basic human tastes. Salt is one of the oldest and most ubiquitous food seasonings, and is known to uniformly improve the taste perception of food. Salting, brining, and pickling are ancient and important methods of food preservation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg/330px-Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt"},{"title":"Lake","blocks":[{"text":"A lake is often a naturally occurring, relatively large and fixed body of water on or near the Earth's surface. It is localized in a basin or interconnected basins surrounded by dry land. Lakes lie completely on land and are separate from the ocean, although they may be connected with the ocean by outflowing rivers. Lakes, like other bodies of water, are part of the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Most lakes are fresh water and account for almost all the world's surface freshwater, but some are salt lakes with salinities even higher than that of seawater. Lakes vary significantly in surface area and volume of water, but in total cover approximately 2.5 × 106 km2 (less than 2%) of the Earth's surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg/330px-Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Saltph26.jpg/500px-Saltph26.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Saltph26.jpg/500px-Saltph26.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Bonneville Salt Flats, Utah ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaltph26.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg/500px-Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg/500px-Cono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cono de Arita in Salar de Arizaro, Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACono_de_Arita_en_el_Salar_de_Arizaro.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Ethiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg/500px-Ethiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Ethiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg/1280px-Ethiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Ethiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg/500px-Ethiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of two salt lakes in the northern end of the Danakil Depression known as Lake Karum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEthiopia_-_Lake_Assale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Lake_Hillier_Shoreline_Pink_Hue_Salt_Deposite.jpg/500px-Lake_Hillier_Shoreline_Pink_Hue_Salt_Deposite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Lake_Hillier_Shoreline_Pink_Hue_Salt_Deposite.jpg/1280px-Lake_Hillier_Shoreline_Pink_Hue_Salt_Deposite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia